AU  - Rosyidin, M
AU  - Rum, M
AU  - Nandyatama, RW
TI  - Norms as a status marker: social creativity and Indonesia's recognition game in the indo-Pacific
T2  - CONTEMPORARY POLITICS
AB  - Recent studies on Indonesia's foreign policy in the Indo-Pacific have been analysed using the concept of middle power diplomacy. Despite its strengths, it lacks subjective dimensions of status since it emphasises the fundamental features of Indonesia's behavioural traits. This paper offers a novel account of Indonesia's vision for the Indo-Pacific by focusing on the ideas of 'dynamic equilibrium' and 'ASEAN Outlook on the Indo-Pacific'. Using Social Identity Theory (SIT), it argues that these concepts represent Indonesia's social creativity strategy in order to be recognised as a regional great power. Rather than reflecting Indonesia's middle power diplomacy, both 'dynamic equilibrium' and 'ASEAN Outlook on the Indo-Pacific' are the country's recognition game to improve its standing in twenty-first- century global politics. By advocating new normative frameworks for Indo-Pacific security architecture, Indonesia avoids demonstrating a revisionist foreign policy, which would elicit a negative response from great powers.
